        {
          "path": "_m/g/b/g/v/porth-nanven-wp/geologically-interesting-beach.jpg",
          "filename": "Geologically interesting beach at Porth Nanven - geograph.org.uk - 229797.jpg",
          "caption": "Geologically interesting beach at Porth Nanven. At the end of the Cot Valley is this National Trust owned beach, shown here in the traditional Cornish drizzle at low tide. The geology of the brown cliff on the left is explained in more detail in this geograph: 71736! A cagouled human figure is supplied in the foreground for scale (crouched down looking into a rockpool). According to the National Trust interpretation board (at the head of the slipway, to the right of the brown cliff) it is an offence to remove stones from this beach.",
          "alt": "Geologically interesting beach at Porth Nanven. At the end of the Cot Valley is this National Trust owned beach, shown here in the traditional Cornish drizzle at low tide. The geology of the brown cliff on the left is explained in more detail in this geograph: 71736! A cagouled human figure is supplied in the foreground for scale (crouched down looking into a rockpool). According to the National Trust interpretation board (at the head of the slipway, to the right of the brown cliff) it is an offence to remove stones from this beach.",
          "width": 640,
          "height": 480,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
          "artist": "Jim Champion",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geologically_interesting_beach_at_Porth_Nanven_-_geograph.org.uk_-_229797.jpg",
